Raise the AVDTP incoming MTU so LDAC can be received.

BlueZ listens for AVDTP connections without asking for an L2CAP incoming MTU,
so the signalling channel — and with it the media transport channel, which
inherits session->imtu in avdtp_connect_cb() — falls back to the L2CAP default
of 672 bytes.  That is the MTU this machine advertises to a sender, and it
becomes the sender's *outgoing* MTU for the media channel.

Sony's LDAC encoder refuses to initialise below 679 bytes:
ldacBT_init_handle_encode() returns LDACBT_ERR_ILL_MTU_SIZE.  A sender that
negotiated LDAC with us therefore cannot start its encoder at all — PipeWire
logs "LDAC encoder initialization failed: LDACBT_ERR_ILL_MTU_SIZE" and streams
nothing, while BlueZ still reports the transport as "active".  Nothing arrives,
and there is no error on the receiving side to explain it.

SBC and AAC pack into far smaller packets, so they work at 672 and hide the
problem; only LDAC fails, which makes this look like a codec bug rather than an
MTU one.

1024 is chosen to match LDACBT_MAX_NBYTES, the largest LDAC transport frame,
leaving the sender free to fill packets rather than fragmenting every frame.
L2CAP splits them to the controller's ACL MTU transparently.

--- a/profiles/audio/a2dp.c
+++ b/profiles/audio/a2dp.c
@@ -70,6 +70,13 @@

 #define AVDTP_PSM 25

+/* L2CAP incoming MTU to advertise for AVDTP.
+ *
+ * The default of 672 is below the 679 byte floor Sony's LDAC encoder accepts,
+ * which silently prevents senders from streaming LDAC to us.  LDACBT_MAX_NBYTES
+ * is 1024, so this holds a whole LDAC transport frame. */
+#define AVDTP_IMTU 1024
+
 #define MEDIA_ENDPOINT_INTERFACE "org.bluez.MediaEndpoint1"

 struct a2dp_sep {
@@ -2346,6 +2353,7 @@ static bool a2dp_server_listen(struct a2
 				BT_IO_OPT_SOURCE_BDADDR,
 				btd_adapter_get_address(server->adapter),
 				BT_IO_OPT_PSM, AVDTP_PSM,
+				BT_IO_OPT_IMTU, AVDTP_IMTU,
 				BT_IO_OPT_MODE, mode,
 				BT_IO_OPT_SEC_LEVEL, BT_IO_SEC_MEDIUM,
 				BT_IO_OPT_MASTER, true,
